WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.140
So what's up with the floating fetus, the black&nbsp;
slab, and the rapidly aging astronaut in 2001:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.540
A Space Odyssey? Stick around to find out!

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.700
It would be an understatement to say&nbsp;
that Stanley Kubrick's 2001: A Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.260
Odyssey, totally changed the game when&nbsp;
it was released way back in 1968. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.760
film's expansive narrative and surrealist&nbsp;
imagery launched thousands of film studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.920
dissertations and helped cement Kubrick as one&nbsp;
of the premier filmmakers of the 20th century.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.720
The narrative of the movie features several&nbsp;
distinct acts. The first takes place before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.820
the dawn of humanity and sees a community&nbsp;
of primates who discover a large, black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.740
rectangular monolith. Although the monolith&nbsp;
appears to be a totally inanimate object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.900
after coming in contact with it, the primates&nbsp;
learn that they can use a large animal bone as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.980
a weapon to fend off another group of primates&nbsp;
who have encroached on their territory.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.720
In the next section, the film flashes forward&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands of years into the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.240
beyond our own time. A group of officials on&nbsp;
a human moon base are investigating a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.100
of abnormalities, and discover a similar&nbsp;
monolith buried in a lunar crater nearby.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.640
The last section of the film, set&nbsp;
a little over a year in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:17.160
centers on a spaceship on a mission to&nbsp;
Jupiter. It's this section that brings 2001:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.040
A Space Odyssey to its famously confusing end.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.380
We're introduced to the crew on a mission&nbsp;
to Jupiter, most of whom are in cryosleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.520
with the exception of Dr. David&nbsp;
Bowman and Dr. Frank Poole. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.000
is one other active crew member&nbsp;
aboard the Discovery One: HAL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.520
a computer that controls the functions of the ship&nbsp;
and communicates with a monotone human male voice.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.860
During the mission, Bowman and Poole become&nbsp;
alarmed when they catch HAL seemingly lying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.840
them about a mechanical problem with one of the&nbsp;
antennas on the outside of the vessel. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.160
with no other recourse, Poole exits&nbsp;
the ship to investigate the issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.400
He learns the hard way that he was&nbsp;
correct to be suspicious of HAL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.720
who sabotages Poole's equipment, killing&nbsp;
him and leaving him floating in space.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:07.020
Bowman exits the ship to try and save Poole,&nbsp;
but it's too late. Not only has HAL deactivated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.520
the life support systems for the crew members&nbsp;
who are still in cryosleep, killing them all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.800
but he also refuses to let Bowman back onto&nbsp;
the ship. This is a shocking and terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.660
development for a number of reasons, perhaps&nbsp;
the most cogent of them being that HAL, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.520
the bone used by the primates in the first section&nbsp;
of the movie, is meant to be a tool for humanity.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.160
But it appears that HAL has a mission of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.180
own. When Bowman tries to re-board&nbsp;
the ship, HAL coldly informs him,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.220
"This mission is too important for&nbsp;
me to allow you to jeopardize it."

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.320
Bowman manages to make his way back&nbsp;
into the ship and begins to manually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.400
shut down HAL. While he does so, the&nbsp;
computer pleads with him to stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.000
promising to stop sabotaging&nbsp;
Bowman, and repeatedly saying,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.240
"I'm afraid, Dave"

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:02.340
But Bowman ignores it. When the computer is fully&nbsp;
deactivated, a video suddenly begins to play. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.200
it, a man sitting at a desk declares that the&nbsp;
video was recorded before the mission began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.120
but is so top-secret that only HAL was&nbsp;
made aware of it. It seems that shutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.140
down HAL caused the video to autoplay,&nbsp;
even though it was clearly meant to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.760
played when the crew reached their destination&nbsp;
in Jupiter's orbit and had all been woken up.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.080
The man in the video then reveals the&nbsp;
true nature of Discovery One's mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.740
to investigate the first sign of&nbsp;
intelligent life in outer space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.060
It was a strong radio emission aimed&nbsp;
at Jupiter, coming from the monolith.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.460
"The four-million-year-old black&nbsp;
monolith has remained completely inert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.120
its origin and purpose still a total mystery."

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.680
What does mission control hope to find&nbsp;
when the ship reaches Jupiter and does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.240
the existence of this video — a profound&nbsp;
piece of existential knowledge uploaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.440
into HAL's mainframe — have anything to&nbsp;
do with the computer's sudden sentience?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.760
Unfortunately for Bowman, he doesn't&nbsp;
have time to piece that puzzle together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.500
as Discovery One is arriving in Jupiter's&nbsp;
orbit, and things are about to get weird.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.200
As the Discovery One approaches Jupiter, it&nbsp;
encounters one of the monoliths orbiting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.820
planet, and then Bowman is pulled into a&nbsp;
slipstream of psychedelic colors for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:20.460
extended sequence. When it ends, he's no longer&nbsp;
on the spaceship and is instead in a classically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:26.340
ornate bedroom suite with futuristic, lighted&nbsp;
floor tiles. He has also aged considerably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.300
a fact that startles him when he walks&nbsp;
into the bathroom and sees himself in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.200
mirror. While in the bathroom, he looks&nbsp;
through a doorway back into the bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.760
where he now sees a man sitting at a table&nbsp;
eating dinner and wearing a black robe.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.680
The man turns around and reveals that he&nbsp;
is Bowman. This Bowman gets up from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.280
table and walks to the bathroom to look&nbsp;
around, as though he heard something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.200
When he returns to the table, he knocks&nbsp;
over a glass. When he turns to pick it up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:57.240
there's now an even older version of Bowman on the&nbsp;
bed in the room, one that is clearly near death.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.340
The older Bowman points to the end of his&nbsp;
bed, and the camera cuts to reveal one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.360
the black monoliths floating in front of&nbsp;
him. When the camera cuts back to Bowman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.180
he has transformed into a fetus, suspended&nbsp;
in a glowing caul over the bed. We then see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.200
the fetus floating in space and observing&nbsp;
earth with a curious look in its eyes.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:22.080
There are lots of theories and interpretations&nbsp;
of 2001: A Space Odyssey's ending. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.920
however, it's clearly a movie that is&nbsp;
meant to be thought about, discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.660
and watched from different points of view. That&nbsp;
being said, there are some obvious threads of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.220
connection between the seemingly separate elements&nbsp;
of the film that help explain what it's all about.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.100
Mankind's harnessing of technology is one of the&nbsp;
clear themes of the movie. The moment the primates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.380
start viewing the bone as a tool can be seen&nbsp;
as a direct precursor to the later sections of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:51.960
the movie that depict man in an advanced space&nbsp;
age. Although these advancements are exciting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.240
they also bring with them serious existential&nbsp;
conundrums, such as the beginning of weaponized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.400
warfare, the discovery of the alien monolith&nbsp;
on the moon, and the emerging sentience of HAL.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.960
"I know I've never completely&nbsp;
freed myself of the suspicion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.100
that there are some extremely&nbsp;
odd things about this mission."

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.680
And perhaps Bowman's rapid aging at the&nbsp;
end of the film is representative of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:21.720
price humanity pays for these advancements.&nbsp;
He is disturbed each time an older version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.580
himself is revealed until he's transformed&nbsp;
into a fetus and exists with tranquility.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.900
This could be read either as his regression&nbsp;
back to a state of blissful ignorance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.020
similar to the primates before&nbsp;
they encountered the monolith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.180
or evolution into some new state of&nbsp;
technological primitivism. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.640
the fetus appears to be in some kind of glowing&nbsp;
orb and floats through space, where it is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.380
to gain a simple, yet powerful, existential&nbsp;
perspective: a view of the earth from space.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.940
Where does that leave the monolith? Is&nbsp;
it a literal piece of alien technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.040
that is influencing humanity's evolution,&nbsp;
or merely symbolic of the terrifyingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.360
inscrutable path our technological&nbsp;
advancements have put us on? Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.080
that is one of those questions each&nbsp;
viewer will have to answer for themselves.

